1
00:00:02,360 --> 00:00:04,977
By now, we had a look at a couple of rules

2
00:00:05,170 --> 00:00:07,867
that you should consider when you pick names

3
00:00:08,060 --> 00:00:11,046
for your variables, functions, or classes.

4
00:00:12,058 --> 00:00:14,547
There also are a couple of pitfalls you

5
00:00:14,547 --> 00:00:17,421
should be aware of, a couple of errors which

6
00:00:17,469 --> 00:00:19,524
are easy to make. Now, here's one thing

7
00:00:19,605 --> 00:00:21,355
which I already mentioned, but it's really

8
00:00:21,387 --> 00:00:23,876
important. Don't include redundant

9
00:00:23,876 --> 00:00:25,128
information in names.

10
00:00:26,107 --> 00:00:29,056
User with name and age is pretty bad if you

11
00:00:29,072 --> 00:00:31,541
create a user like this because it's kind of

12
00:00:31,797 --> 00:00:35,067
obvious what's in this user. And if we would

13
00:00:35,099 --> 00:00:37,392
wanna stick with this pattern, we could come

14
00:00:37,504 --> 00:00:40,438
up with super long variable names if that

15
00:00:40,454 --> 00:00:42,922
user would not just have a name and an age,

16
00:00:43,435 --> 00:00:46,705
but maybe also hobbies, a role, a first and

17
00:00:46,785 --> 00:00:49,879
a last name, and so on. You get the point.

18
00:00:50,873 --> 00:00:52,845
So this is too specific.

19
00:00:53,438 --> 00:00:56,804
Yes, names have the purpose of making us

20
00:00:56,804 --> 00:00:59,308
understand code without diving into the

21
00:00:59,308 --> 00:01:02,054
details of a certain implementation. But

22
00:01:02,118 --> 00:01:04,190
that does not mean that we wanna describe

23
00:01:04,431 --> 00:01:07,064
everything with a variable name. This is too

24
00:01:07,161 --> 00:01:09,794
much information which doesn't help us. In

25
00:01:09,987 --> 00:01:12,363
most code snippets, it's probably enough to

26
00:01:12,395 --> 00:01:14,627
quickly understand that we're storing a

27
00:01:14,627 --> 00:01:17,438
user, not that it's a user with name and

28
00:01:17,518 --> 00:01:17,662
age.

29
00:01:18,642 --> 00:01:21,420
If we wanna access that information, modern

30
00:01:21,613 --> 00:01:24,535
IDEs and editors will give us autocompletion

31
00:01:24,664 --> 00:01:26,943
anyways. And we can also simply expect

32
00:01:26,943 --> 00:01:29,896
certain fields to be in that object given

33
00:01:29,896 --> 00:01:32,110
the fact that it's named user. It's very

34
00:01:32,142 --> 00:01:33,715
likely to have a name after all.

35
00:01:34,614 --> 00:01:38,257
Hence, better names here would be just user

36
00:01:38,706 --> 00:01:41,515
or if we wanna be more specific because it

37
00:01:41,515 --> 00:01:44,789
makes sense, maybe it's a newUser because

38
00:01:44,885 --> 00:01:47,757
maybe this code gets executed in some part

39
00:01:47,870 --> 00:01:51,160
of our code where we are only operating on a

40
00:01:51,160 --> 00:01:53,968
newly created user and that information

41
00:01:53,968 --> 00:01:54,947
might be important.

42
00:01:55,750 --> 00:01:58,192
Or maybe it's a logged-in user and we wanna

43
00:01:58,208 --> 00:02:00,537
reflect that and we wanna make sure that

44
00:02:00,553 --> 00:02:02,850
everyone reading the code knows that for

45
00:02:02,962 --> 00:02:05,500
this user, you don't need to check whether

46
00:02:05,516 --> 00:02:08,006
he or she is logged in, for example.

47
00:02:08,568 --> 00:02:10,784
These could be variations which make sense.

48
00:02:11,379 --> 00:02:14,013
User with name and age doesn't make too much

49
00:02:14,125 --> 00:02:14,414
sense.

50
00:02:15,555 --> 00:02:19,635
Also avoid slang, unclear abbreviations, and

51
00:02:19,667 --> 00:02:20,791
disinformation.

52
00:02:21,753 --> 00:02:24,109
You wanna avoid slang like this, for

53
00:02:24,142 --> 00:02:27,027
example. It might be funny to you, it might

54
00:02:27,027 --> 00:02:29,752
make sense to you, maybe also to you and

55
00:02:29,768 --> 00:02:33,343
your team, but overall it's not too helpful.

56
00:02:33,599 --> 00:02:36,629
And if I dive into this project and I have

57
00:02:36,645 --> 00:02:38,649
not been part of the team right from the

58
00:02:38,649 --> 00:02:41,711
start, I don't immediately understand what

59
00:02:41,711 --> 00:02:42,881
these methods are doing.

60
00:02:43,714 --> 00:02:46,520
Die please, for example, sounds like the

61
00:02:46,520 --> 00:02:49,549
product is about to be deleted, but I can't

62
00:02:49,549 --> 00:02:52,819
be 100% sure about that unless I dive into

63
00:02:52,852 --> 00:02:53,221
code.

64
00:02:53,751 --> 00:02:56,913
So instead, don't use slang. Use objective,

65
00:02:57,009 --> 00:03:00,284
clear names like remove or sendErrorMessage

66
00:03:00,316 --> 00:03:00,557
here.

67
00:03:01,343 --> 00:03:03,912
Unclear abbreviations can also be a big

68
00:03:03,912 --> 00:03:04,313
issue.

69
00:03:05,116 --> 00:03:08,214
Consider these examples here. messageN

70
00:03:08,888 --> 00:03:13,062
and then YMDT equals something. The second

71
00:03:13,062 --> 00:03:16,834
line is probably some date snapshot, but

72
00:03:16,834 --> 00:03:19,210
it's hard to reason about it. It's not clear

73
00:03:19,403 --> 00:03:21,505
what is what. At least not immediately.

74
00:03:22,227 --> 00:03:24,233
So here, clearer names help a lot.

75
00:03:24,811 --> 00:03:27,507
If we use newUser instead of n, it's clear

76
00:03:27,507 --> 00:03:29,818
that we're sending a message to a new user

77
00:03:30,011 --> 00:03:31,006
and not to something.

78
00:03:31,696 --> 00:03:34,376
And if we name the variable dateWithTimeZone

79
00:03:34,649 --> 00:03:37,120
or something like that, it's also much

80
00:03:37,297 --> 00:03:39,544
clearer what's inside of that variable.

81
00:03:40,426 --> 00:03:42,705
Last but not least, disinformation should

82
00:03:42,834 --> 00:03:45,642
also be avoided. And I have seen code like

83
00:03:45,642 --> 00:03:48,081
this. We have all seen code like this. We

84
00:03:48,081 --> 00:03:50,633
maybe even wrote code like this. What's the

85
00:03:50,633 --> 00:03:51,370
problem here?

86
00:03:52,316 --> 00:03:54,529
Well, the first line of code has a variable

87
00:03:54,561 --> 00:03:57,961
named user_list, but this isn't a list. It's

88
00:03:58,009 --> 00:04:01,264
a JavaScript object or close to a Python

89
00:04:01,296 --> 00:04:03,782
dictionary. It's definitely not an array.

90
00:04:04,022 --> 00:04:05,578
There are no square brackets.

91
00:04:06,813 --> 00:04:10,148
All accounts implies that we have, well, all

92
00:04:10,517 --> 00:04:11,126
accounts.

93
00:04:11,720 --> 00:04:13,852
But instead, it looks like we're filtering

94
00:04:13,965 --> 00:04:16,931
for certain accounts. So by definition, we

95
00:04:17,060 --> 00:04:20,073
probably don't have all accounts. At least

96
00:04:20,073 --> 00:04:20,810
it's misleading.

97
00:04:21,804 --> 00:04:24,881
So better names would be userMap or just

98
00:04:25,009 --> 00:04:28,311
user if we store some object or map in that

99
00:04:28,311 --> 00:04:29,930
variable instead of a list.

100
00:04:30,539 --> 00:04:33,023
And filteredAccounts makes more sense than

101
00:04:33,023 --> 00:04:34,017
accountsFilter.

102
00:04:34,946 --> 00:04:36,790
Of course, depending on the application

103
00:04:36,790 --> 00:04:38,905
we're writing, we might also be able to

104
00:04:38,969 --> 00:04:41,662
replace filteredAccounts with a better name.

105
00:04:42,399 --> 00:04:44,819
Maybe it's just the user accounts who have a

106
00:04:44,819 --> 00:04:45,509
paid plan.

107
00:04:46,102 --> 00:04:48,475
Then we could name it paidAccounts, making

108
00:04:48,507 --> 00:04:51,073
it clear for what we're filtering. But it's

109
00:04:51,217 --> 00:04:53,639
all better than allAccounts for sure.

110
00:04:54,906 --> 00:04:56,798
Also make sure that you work with

111
00:04:56,911 --> 00:04:58,097
distinctive names.

112
00:04:58,691 --> 00:05:01,641
Let's say you work with some Analytics class

113
00:05:01,641 --> 00:05:03,774
which you used to create an Analytics

114
00:05:03,967 --> 00:05:04,480
object.

115
00:05:05,025 --> 00:05:07,399
And now this class exposes a couple of

116
00:05:07,415 --> 00:05:10,879
methods to you. getDailyData and you pass a

117
00:05:10,879 --> 00:05:13,286
day into the method getDayData,

118
00:05:13,607 --> 00:05:16,337
getRawDailyData with a day argument,

119
00:05:16,642 --> 00:05:19,323
getParsedDailyData with a day argument.

120
00:05:19,949 --> 00:05:21,121
What are these things doing?

121
00:05:22,278 --> 00:05:23,450
Don't write code like this.

122
00:05:24,108 --> 00:05:26,565
These methods all sound very familiar.

123
00:05:27,335 --> 00:05:29,102
It's very hard to tell what the different

124
00:05:29,102 --> 00:05:31,687
methods do and which method you wanna use.

125
00:05:32,136 --> 00:05:34,529
And therefore, again, if you are the person

126
00:05:34,641 --> 00:05:37,018
diving into that code or working with that

127
00:05:37,050 --> 00:05:39,391
code, You have to dive into these methods

128
00:05:39,455 --> 00:05:41,635
first to understand which method you now

129
00:05:41,699 --> 00:05:44,120
need to call or what could be causing a

130
00:05:44,184 --> 00:05:44,985
certain bug.

131
00:05:46,043 --> 00:05:48,817
So instead use distinctive names like

132
00:05:48,977 --> 00:05:51,878
getDailyReport if this maybe doesn't return

133
00:05:51,878 --> 00:05:55,116
the data but some PDF or Microsoft Excel

134
00:05:55,245 --> 00:05:55,742
report.

135
00:05:56,367 --> 00:05:58,739
getDataForToday makes more sense than

136
00:05:58,852 --> 00:06:00,967
getDayData because it's clear that it's the

137
00:06:01,016 --> 00:06:01,737
data for today.

138
00:06:02,458 --> 00:06:05,023
getRawDailyData makes sense but now it

139
00:06:05,023 --> 00:06:07,075
doesn't clash with the other names because

140
00:06:07,123 --> 00:06:09,095
the other names are very clear and

141
00:06:09,175 --> 00:06:09,560
distinctive

142
00:06:10,780 --> 00:06:12,676
So now it's very easy to tell what the

143
00:06:12,724 --> 00:06:13,720
different methods do

144
00:06:14,250 --> 00:06:16,145
and therefore working with this code becomes

145
00:06:16,241 --> 00:06:16,980
much easier.

146
00:06:18,409 --> 00:06:21,477
Now the last pitfall or the last thing, the

147
00:06:21,477 --> 00:06:23,725
last concept to be aware of is that you

148
00:06:23,725 --> 00:06:26,825
should stay consistent when naming things.

149
00:06:27,644 --> 00:06:30,021
For example, let's say you are writing a

150
00:06:30,069 --> 00:06:33,025
function which retrieves some data, let's

151
00:06:33,025 --> 00:06:34,133
say a list of users.

152
00:06:34,872 --> 00:06:37,265
You could name that function getUsers,

153
00:06:37,602 --> 00:06:39,992
fetchUsers, or retrieveUsers.

154
00:06:40,585 --> 00:06:43,455
They all kind of describe the same thing and

155
00:06:43,695 --> 00:06:45,362
all 3 names would be fine.

156
00:06:46,116 --> 00:06:47,847
You can go with either of these options

157
00:06:48,520 --> 00:06:49,819
but then stick with it.

158
00:06:50,428 --> 00:06:52,929
Be consistent. Throughout your entire

159
00:06:52,993 --> 00:06:56,311
program, always use the same pattern. If you

160
00:06:56,439 --> 00:06:59,677
went with getUsers in one place, also use

161
00:06:59,822 --> 00:07:02,643
getProducts in another place and don't have

162
00:07:02,771 --> 00:07:05,977
fetchProducts in one class and getUsers in

163
00:07:05,977 --> 00:07:08,494
another class. It will not be obvious if

164
00:07:08,558 --> 00:07:10,433
these methods do generally the

165
00:07:11,299 --> 00:07:12,935
or if there is a key difference between

166
00:07:13,047 --> 00:07:15,469
getting and fetching in your program.

167
00:07:16,511 --> 00:07:19,302
So be consistent and stick to the names you

168
00:07:19,302 --> 00:07:19,703
go with.

169
00:07:20,328 --> 00:07:22,044
If you then decide that you don't like the

170
00:07:22,044 --> 00:07:24,145
name, you can always change it, but it will

171
00:07:24,257 --> 00:07:27,368
probably not be the get, fetch, or retrieve

172
00:07:27,400 --> 00:07:29,549
part which you don't like, but the part

173
00:07:29,549 --> 00:07:29,998
thereafter.

174
00:07:30,992 --> 00:07:33,286
And that's really it. These are the core

175
00:07:33,638 --> 00:07:36,220
rules, the core ideas and concepts, and the

176
00:07:36,220 --> 00:07:39,027
core pitfalls and problems you should be

177
00:07:39,027 --> 00:07:42,131
aware of and you should consider when naming

178
00:07:42,244 --> 00:07:42,697
things.

179
00:07:43,296 --> 00:07:45,432
So therefore, let's now dive into a couple

180
00:07:45,464 --> 00:07:47,923
of demos and of course also your challenge.
